Generated operation description:
 `regexr_transform`

The purpose is to identify and categorize businesses as either "in business" or "out of business". To achieve this, we will apply a regex pattern to standardize the different ways "out of business" is represented. The operation will convert all instances of "Out of Business", "[Out of Business]", "OOB", "out of business", and "OUT OF BUSINESS" into a consistent format, such as "OutOfBusiness".

Here's the code:

```python
import re
def standardize_business(value):
    pattern = r'(?i)([Oo]ut\s+of\s+b(u|us)iness)|(?-i)[Oo](B|b)U(S|s)S|(?-i)[Oo](B|b)O(B|b)?|(?-i)[Oo](B|b)\[(Oo)(B|b)U(S|s)S\]'
    return re.sub(pattern, 'OutOfBusiness', value)
```

Output:

```
standardize_business('Fail')                   # 'Fail'
standardize_business('out of business;')         # 'OutOfBusiness'
standardize_business('Pass')                    # 'Pass'
standardize_business('Pass')                    # 'Pass'
standardize_business('[Out of Business]')        # 'OutOfBusiness'
standardize_business('Fail')                    # 'Fail'
standardize_business('Out of Business')         # 'OutOfBusiness'
standardize_business('OOB')                      # 'OutOfBusiness'
standardize_business('OUT OF BUSINESS')          # 'OutOfBusiness'
standardize_business('Pass')                    # 'Pass'
```
